### Findings

- **F02-1** [minor] `crates/c3/src/budget/mod.rs:436` - The new z.ai quota GET builds a default ureq agent (follows up to 5 redirects) with `Authorization: Bearer <key>` attached, so a redirect from api.z.ai would be followed — unlike maintain/env.rs, which pins redirects(0) and re-targets by hand, same-site only, for keyless GETs; the wave's stated invariant is that a request carrying an API key never follows a redirect. Trigger: api.z.ai (or an intermediary) answering GET /api/monitor/usage/quota/limit with a 3xx to another host while the ZAI key is set. Evidence: read-code: Both build `ureq::AgentBuilder::new().timeout(HTTP_TIMEOUT).build()` with no redirects(0) and send the bearer token; contrast crates/c3/src/maintain/env.rs:122-157 which sets .redirects(0) and follows only keyless GETs, max 3 hops, same site.; assumed: Not verified whether ureq's default strips Authorization on cross-host redirects; the follow itself is certain. Verify: Inspect the vendored ureq 2.12.1 source (redirect_auth_headers default) or point the ZAI env key at a local server that 302s cross-host and observe whether the Authorization header arrives. Remedy: Build the agent with .redirects(0) (and treat a 3xx as no data) in zai_get and kimi_get, matching the maintain env policy.
- **F02-2** [minor] `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:507`, `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:581` - When the installed version cannot be parsed (`installed = None`, text "unknown"), the pass still updates and, on a post-update smoke failure, rolls back to the literal string "unknown" (npm install -g @openai/codex@unknown, grok update --version unknown), which cannot succeed — the engine is left on the version that failed its smoke although a rollback path existed. Trigger: A CLI whose `--version` output stops parsing (format change across versions is exactly when this happens) plus an available update whose post-update smoke fails. Evidence: read-code: The up-to-date check is inside `if let Some(inst) = installed`, so None falls through to update with installed_text="unknown"; do_rollback receives installed_text verbatim. Verify: Point CODEX_CONSULT_GROK_EXE at a fake whose --version prints no version token, latest > installed, smoke pass-then-fail: the pass reports "rollback failed" and the engine stays on the new version. Remedy: Hold the update (report "installed version unknown") whenever `installed` is None for an engine with a rollback path.
- **F02-3** [minor] `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:624` - The model step ignores both the `--engine` filter and C3_MAINTAIN_SKIP: `c3 maintain --run --engine codex` still collects and swaps models of grok/agy/claude seats, editing the roster and spending smoke turns outside the scope the operator selected. Trigger: Any scoped or skip-configured pass (`--engine codex` or C3_MAINTAIN_SKIP=grok) with a roster seat whose source has a successor. Evidence: read-code: run_models receives `opts` but reads only opts.dry_run; `skip` and `only` are computed in pass_inner and never passed to it. Verify: With a roster holding a swappable grok seat, run `c3 maintain --run --dry-run --engine codex` and observe grok model candidate lines in the output. Remedy: Filter swap candidates (and collect sources) by opts.engines and skip_set inside run_models.
- **F02-4** [minor] `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:651`, `crates/c3/src/maintain/smoke.rs:37` - A model-swap smoke builds its Target directly from the candidate instead of going through pick_target, so the saturation guard (no plan whose governing window is at 90 %+ used) and the budget-headroom/codex-custom-provider preference never apply to swap smokes — they can spend quota on an exhausted plan. Trigger: A swap candidate exists on a seat whose plan window is at or beyond 90 % used at pass time. Evidence: read-code: The saturation check and ranking exist only inside pick_target; the swap path constructs Target { provider, model } inline and calls run_one_smoke directly. Verify: Set the seat's plan snapshot to 95 % used, offer a successor id, and run a non-dry pass: the smoke runs anyway. Remedy: Route the swap target through pick_target's plan_headroom check (or skip the swap when the seat's plan is saturated).
- **F02-5** [note] `crates/c3/src/consult/orchestrate/secondary.rs:1471`, `crates/c3/src/engines/grok.rs:740` - The dead-re-ask salvage concatenates ALL text events, so the reply recorded for a run whose re-ask read and then died can still contain the interim placeholder written before the last tool call — inconsistent with grok_turn_outcome's final-text-after-last-tool-call rule the same change introduces. Trigger: A grok re-ask that emits an answer-shaped interim note, makes tool calls, emits partial final text, then dies. Evidence: read-code: grok_salvage pushes every `text` event; the outcome path keeps only final_text after the last tool_call. Verify: Script a grok re-ask (fake stream) with a placeholder, a tool_call, then partial text and no end event: the .reply.json contains both the placeholder and the partial text. Remedy: Make grok_salvage return the text after the last tool_call (fall back to all text only when there was none).
- **F02-6** [note] `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:265` - The in-use guard is a one-shot match of process names against the launcher's file stem, checked before two smokes that take minutes: npm-shimmed CLIs may run under a different image name (node / codex-<target>) so a live consultation may not be detected, and there is a TOCTOU window between the check and do_update. Trigger: A consultation on the engine starts while the pass is running its pre-update smoke, or the engine's real process name differs from the launcher stem. Evidence: read-code: engine_in_use compares lowercased process-name stems; it runs once per engine before maintain_one_engine, which then smokes twice before updating.; inferred: Windows npm shims (.cmd) launch the package binary whose image name may not equal the shim stem; consequence is bounded (Windows file locks make the update fail cleanly; POSIX keeps the old inode). Verify: Start a real `c3 consult --engine grok` and, while it runs, run `c3 maintain --run --engine grok` in a sandbox: check whether the pass reports "skipped: in use". Remedy: Re-check engine_in_use immediately before do_update, and match on the launcher's resolved binary path as well as the stem.
- **F02-7** [note] `crates/c3/src/maintain/models.rs:254` - apply_swap writes the roster with a bare fs::write (no temp+rename), so a crash mid-write can leave a truncated roster file; the same-day .bak-maintain-<date> backup stands, but recovery is manual. Separately, two seats sharing the same provider AND model value can never be swapped (replace_entry_model returns an ambiguity error) — safe, but a silent no-op. Trigger: Power loss / kill during the roster write; or a roster with duplicate provider+model seats offered a successor. Evidence: read-code: State files use tmp+rename (mod.rs:94-107) but the roster write does not; the disambiguation filter keeps every match when both entries name the provider. Verify: Kill the process between the backup and the write (or construct duplicate seats) and inspect the roster / the pass report. Remedy: Write the roster via temp file + rename like write_state; consider disambiguating duplicate seats by position.

